; The man behind the north window (Stage 1.1). Authored in Blender by
; tools/build_window_figure_blender.py, imported by tools/import_window_figure.gd.
;
; Why four meshes and not one. The GDD 10 contract needs the head to move on its
; own - a permanent twenty degree list, and a four degree tick after 1.8 s of
; being looked at - while the body stays dead still. So Coat hangs off
; VisualIndicator, and Head and HatBrim are two children of VisualIndicator that
; share one neck pivot and are always written together. EyeGlints is parented
; under Head, so the four facets in the sockets travel with the face.
;
; The contract names other code depends on:
;   VisualIndicator   what is shown and hidden, and whose subtree defines the
;                     silhouette AABB the frustum test uses
;   Coat              MeshInstance3D, authored ArrayMesh, the standing body
;   Head              MeshInstance3D, authored ArrayMesh, at the neck pivot
;   HatBrim           MeshInstance3D, authored ArrayMesh, at the same pivot
; and there is no ShimmerLight and no Light3D anywhere in this subtree: a light
; here would be a lamp standing in the yard, and the yard has had none since the
; mine shut in 1885.
;
; The meshes are authored with the boot soles at y = -0.94 in figure-local space,
; so the node's own origin stands on the ground wherever it is placed - the
; office and the title diorama both put its feet on the yard at y = 0.96.
;
; use_stage_positions stays OFF here: the title diorama places this scene by hand
; and must not be walked to Stage B. The office turns it on in its own instance.

